T* (***** File GIOTTO.TXT                                                                                                             ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (NOTE: This file was created by scanning the original hardcopy article                                                             ) Tj
T* (and only the Figure captions are included.                                                                                        ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (The Giotto Project                                                                                                                ) Tj
T* (R.   Reinhard                                                                                                                     ) Tj
T* (ESA Space Science Department, ESTEC, Noordwijk,                                                                                   ) Tj
T* (The Netherlands                                                                                                                   ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (Abstract                                                                                                                          ) Tj
T* (  ESA's Giotto Mission to Halley's Comet is a fast flyby at 00 UT on 14 March 1986,                                               ) Tj
T* (about four weeks after the comet's perihelion passage, when it will have been at its                                              ) Tj
T* (most active. The scientific payload is made up of ten experiments with a total mass                                               ) Tj
T* (of approximately 60 kg: a camera for imaging the comet nucleus, three mass spec-                                                  ) Tj
T* (trometers for analyzing the elemental and isotopic composition of the cometary dust                                               ) Tj
T* (and gas environment, various dust impact detectors, a photopolarimeter for measuring                                              ) Tj
T* (the coma's brightness, and a set of plasma instruments for studying the solar-                                                    ) Tj
T* (wind/comet interaction. In view of the high flyby velocity of 68 km/s, the active ex-                                             ) Tj
T* (periment time will only be very short - of the order of 4 h - and all data are to be                                              ) Tj
T* (transmitted back to Earth in real time, at a rate of 40 kbit/s.                                                                   ) Tj
T* (  The Giotto spacecraft is spin-stabilized and has a despun, high-gain parabolic dish                                             ) Tj
T* (antenna inclined at 44.3deg which will point towards Earth during the cometary en-                                                ) Tj
T* (counter. A specially designed 'bumper shield' protects the front end of the spacecraft                                            ) Tj
T* (from being destroyed by hyper-velocity dust impacts.                                                                              ) Tj
T* (  The Giotto spacecraft will be targetted to pass the nucleus at a distance of 500 km                                             ) Tj
T* (on the sunward side.                                                                                                              ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (1. Introduction                                                                                                                   ) Tj
T* (  The Giotto project was approved in July 1980, allowing precisely five years between                                             ) Tj
T* (approval and launch. During the Giotto mission study phase, a Scientific Working                                                  ) Tj
T* (Group formulated the scientific objectives and defined a mission concept and a model                                              ) Tj
T* (payload. The scientific objectives for the Giotto mission are:                                                                    ) Tj
T* (-   to ascertain the elemental and isotopic composition of the volatile components in                                             ) Tj
T* (    the cometary coma, in particular to identify the parent molecules                                                             ) Tj
T* (-   to characterize the physical processes and chemical reactions that occur in the                                               ) Tj
T* (    cometary atmosphere and ionosphere                                                                                            ) Tj
T* (-   to determine the elemental and isotopic composition of the cometary dust particles                                            ) Tj
T* (-   to measure the total gas production rate and the dust flux and size/mass distribu-                                            ) Tj
T* (    tion and to derive the dust-to-gas ratio                                                                                      ) Tj
T* (-   to investigate the macroscopic system of plasma flows resulting from the interac-                                             ) Tj
T* (    tion between the cometary and the solar-wind plasma                                                                           ) Tj
T* (-   to provide numerous images of the comet nucleus with a resolution down to                                                     ) Tj
T* (    50 m, thereby allowing the nucleus' size and rotation to be deduced and its mass                                              ) Tj
T* (    to be estimated.                                                                                                              ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (To accomplish these objectives, the Giotto spacecraft carries ten scientific                                                      ) Tj
T* (experiments, selected in January 1981, some of which have more than one sensor.                                                   ) Tj
T* (They are listed in Table 1 together with their mass, power and data-rate allocations.                                             ) Tj
T* (  An intense Earth-based observation programme forms a natural and necessary com-                                                 ) Tj
T* (plement to the Giotto mission.                                                                                                    ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (2. The scientific payload                                                                                                         ) Tj
T* (2.1 The Halley Multicolour Camera \(HMC\)                                                                                           ) Tj
T* (  The camera is designed to detect and image the nucleus of Comet Halley, to measure                                              ) Tj
T* (the nucleus' size, shape and albedo, and to observe its active sublimation process. By                                            ) Tj
T* (following the moving comet nucleus, the camera's microcomputers can determine the                                                 ) Tj
T* (spacecraft's trajectory parameters relative to the nucleus in real time.                                                          ) Tj
ET
endstream
