T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (2.2 The Neutral Mass Spectrometer \(NMS\)                                                                                           ) Tj
T* (  As the spacecraft flies through the cometary coma, cometary neutrals will be en-                                                ) Tj
T* (countered at the flyby velocity of 68 km/s. Since the gas outflow velocity is much                                                ) Tj
T* (smaller \(<1 km/s\), one might think that the mass of the cometary neutrals could be                                                ) Tj
T* (determined simply by measuring their energy \(since E= 1/2 mv**2\). However, the                                                    ) Tj
T* (daughter molecules can derive significant kinetic energies from energetic interactions                                            ) Tj
T* (such as photo-dissociation and ion-molecular reactions, which appear either as a                                                  ) Tj
T* (change in the incident direction, or as an increase or decrease in energy, e.g. an oxygen                                         ) Tj
T* (atom with a velocity of 5 km/s can appear anywhere between mass 14 and 18. Conse-                                                 ) Tj
T* (quently, an energy spectrum will be smeared, predominantly in the lower mass ranges,                                              ) Tj
T* (and individual peaks corresponding to particular masses cannot be resolved. In the                                                ) Tj
T* (higher mass ranges, the cometary neutral velocity distribution will be 'cold' and the                                             ) Tj
T* (energy spectrum will correspond to the mass spectrum.                                                                             ) Tj
T* (  In the lower mass range \(1-36 amu\), therefore, a double-focusing \(angle and                                                     ) Tj
T* (energy\) mass spectrometer is used consisting of a parallel-plate electrostatic energy                                             ) Tj
T* (analyzer followed by a magnetic sector field momentum analyzer. The particle beam                                                 ) Tj
T* (is imaged onto a microchannel plate with linear readout, where each position cor-                                                 ) Tj
T* (responds to a particular mass. The energy information that is lost in the 'M-analyzer'                                            ) Tj
T* (is provided by the ''E-analyzer', which is simply a parallel-plate electrostatic analyzer                                         ) Tj
T* (with single focusing \(angle\) properties. Again, the particle beam is imaged onto a                                                ) Tj
T* (microchannel plate with linear readout, where each position corresponds to a par-                                                 ) Tj
T* (ticular energy. For analysis, the beam of cometary neutrals has first to be transformed                                           ) Tj
T* (into a beam of ions; this is achieved by bombardment with an electron beam, with the                                              ) Tj
T* (electrons being emitted from either of two redundant filaments.                                                                   ) Tj
T* (  Both sensors \(Figs.2a,b\) have separate and nearly identical gas inlet systems with                                              ) Tj
T* (an electron beam ion source of fly-through geometry. The ion source operates in two                                               ) Tj
T* (modes. In the neutral mode, cometary ions are reflected by deflecting plates in front                                             ) Tj
T* (of the entrance slit; in the ion mode, the deflecting plates are switched off and the elec-                                       ) Tj
T* (tron emission is suppressed, allowing cometary ions to enter the analyzer. Further                                                ) Tj
T* (away from the comet nucleus, the instrument will measure predominantly ions, close                                                ) Tj
T* (to the nucleus predominantly neutrals.                                                                                            ) Tj
