T* (  The Giotto thermal design makes use of both active and passive techniques. The                                                  ) Tj
T* (passive means are radiators under the high-gain antenna and between the main and the                                              ) Tj
T* (experiment platform using Optical Surface Reflectors, and Multi-Layer Insulation                                                  ) Tj
T* (blankets covering the space between the solar-cell array and the experiment platform.                                             ) Tj
T* (The active means are heaters which can be activated by ground command or by                                                       ) Tj
T* (dedicated onboard software, three identical shutters \(variable-area radiators\), which                                             ) Tj
T* (can also be activated by ground command, 12 diode boards which use some of the                                                    ) Tj
T* (solar array's power to heat the enclosure during some cold phases, and commutation                                                ) Tj
T* (of the excess power generated by the solar-cell array in some External Power Dumper                                               ) Tj
T* (phases to the Internal Power Dumper, which is a circuit heating the TWTAs and a                                                   ) Tj
T* (portion of the upper platform during critical phases. Finally, Giotto makes extensive                                             ) Tj
T* (use \(several m**2\) of a new thermal-control coating, the electrically conductive white                                            ) Tj
T* (paint PCB-Z \(absorptivity alpha =0.25, emissivity epsilon =0.82\).                                                                 ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (4.  The mission                                                                                                                   ) Tj
T* (  The Giotto mission is a fast flyby of Comet Halley around midnight UT on 13 March                                               ) Tj
T* (1986, near the comet's post-perihelion crossing of the ecliptic plane, about 1 month                                              ) Tj
T* (after its perihelion passage. At this time, the comet will be at its most active. A pre-                                          ) Tj
T* (perihelion encounter was also considered at an early stage, but was not further pur-                                              ) Tj
T* (sued, primarily because the launch energy needed would have been much higher,                                                     ) Tj
T* (which for a given launcher would have imposed a severe limitation on the mass of the                                              ) Tj
T* (scientific payload that could be launched.                                                                                        ) Tj
T* ( After five years of development and testing, Giotto was launched on 2 July 1985                                                  ) Tj
T* (by an Ariane-1 rocket from Kourou, French Guyana. The spacecraft was initially in-                                                ) Tj
T* (jected into a Geostationary Transfer Orbit \(perigee: 198.5 km, apogee: 36000 km,                                                  ) Tj
T* (inclination 7deg\). After three revolutions in this orbit, the onboard boost motor was fired                                       ) Tj
T* (near perigee to inject Giotto into a heliocentric orbit. The high-gain antenna was                                                ) Tj
T* (despun three days later.                                                                                                          ) Tj
T* (  The camera was switched on in Format 3 on 10 August 1985 to monitor the declam-                                                 ) Tj
T* (ping of its barrel, followed by the Magnetometer Experiment and Energetic Particles                                               ) Tj
T* (Experiment switch-on on 22 August. These two experiments have remained on ever                                                    ) Tj
T* (since, using their memories to bridge gaps in ground-station coverage. The complete                                               ) Tj
T* (switch-on/pyro firing sequence is shown in Table 11. All experiment functional tests                                              ) Tj
T* (have already been performed successfully.                                                                                         ) Tj
T* (  During its cruise phase, the Giotto spacecraft is being controlled by a number of                                               ) Tj
T* (ground stations distributed around the globe. Depending on their size and equipment,                                              ) Tj
T* (these ground stations are used for different purposes and different mission phases                                                ) Tj
T* (\(Table 12\). ESA's station at Carnarvon is the prime station for spacecraft commanding                                             ) Tj
T* (throughout the entire mission. Format-3 data can be received by Carnavon until mid-                                               ) Tj
T* (January, and by Weilheim until mid-February. The NASA 34 m Deep-Space Network                                                     ) Tj
T* (\(Goldstone, Madrid & Canberra\) is being used in addition for spacecraft ranging dur-                                              ) Tj
T* (ing the cruise phase.                                                                                                             ) Tj
T* (  Throughout the mission, the spacecraft attitude defined by the Sun Aspect Angle                                                 ) Tj
T* (\(SAA\) \(0deg = Sun above the tripod\) must be kept inside an allowed corridor \(Fig. 17\),                                            ) Tj
T* (which is defined by two constraints: the high-gain antenna must point permanently at                                              ) Tj
T* (the Earth to maintain the telecommunications link, and the spacecraft must generate                                               ) Tj
T* (enough power without being heated up too much \(thermal/power constraint\). The                                                     ) Tj
T* (hotest phase of the mission occurred at the end of December 1985, when Giotto was                                                 ) Tj
T* (only 0.72 AU away from the Sun.                                                                                                   ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (Figure 17. Sun Aspect Angle from launch until                                                                                     ) Tj
T* (encounter. The solid line shows the strategy that                                                                                 ) Tj
T* (is actually being followed; the dashed lines                                                                                      ) Tj
T* (represent various constraints \(see text for details\)                                                                              ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
T* (                                                                                                                                  ) Tj
