T* (flicting requirements of three groups of experiments. One experiment group \(HMC\)                                                  ) Tj
T* (would like to fly by at 1000 km, but no closer than 500 km; a second group \(OPE\),                                                 ) Tj
T* (MAG, NMS, IMS, GRE\) would like to fly by the nucleus as close as possible, even                                                   ) Tj
T* (if the spacecraft would not survive; while a third group \(PIA, DID, EPA, JPA, RPA\)                                                ) Tj
T* (would also like to fly by as close as possible whilst still maintaining a high survival                                           ) Tj
T* (probability.                                                                                                                      ) Tj
T* (  Targeting the spacecraft to this preselected point is a difficult task as the nucleus                                           ) Tj
T* (is too small to be observed from the Earth even by the largest telescopes and moreover                                            ) Tj
T* (is disguised by the coma dust and gas. Also, due to the asymmetric outgassing - the                                               ) Tj
T* (dust and gas is mostly emitted into the sunward hemisphere - the nucleus is ac-                                                   ) Tj
T* (celerated away from the Sun \(nongravitational forces\). Compared with the purely                                                   ) Tj
T* (Keplerian orbit that it follows at large heliocentric distances, Halley 's orbit is extended                                      ) Tj
T* (by four days. This outgassing effect is irregular and therefore difficult to model. Bet-                                          ) Tj
T* (ween Halley's recovery on 16 October 1982 and mid-January 1986,4000 astrometric                                                   ) Tj
T* (observations were made of Halley. They are being coordinated via the Astrometry Net                                               ) Tj
T* (of the International Halley Watch. From these observations, in combination with a                                                 ) Tj
T* (model which includes the effects of nongravitational perturbations, the nucleus' posi-                                            ) Tj
T* (tion can be determined for the time of the Giotto encounter with an accuracy of 236                                               ) Tj
T* (km in a radial direction from the Sun, 989 km in the direction of the comet's orbital                                             ) Tj
T* (motion and 15 km perpendicular to the comet's orbital plane \(these are 1 sigma values\).                                           ) Tj
T* (In calculating these values, an offset of 500 km between the centre of brightness                                                 ) Tj
T* (\(presumed to be the nucleus position\) and the centre of mass \(actual nucleus position\)                                            ) Tj
T* (has been assumed, which is probably too pessimistic. For targeting purposes, the 1 sigma                                          ) Tj
T* (uncertainty ellipsoid reduces to an ellipse in the target plane, defined by the Giotto                                            ) Tj
T* (arrival direction. With the same assumption on the offset, its two components are 231                                             ) Tj
T* (and 131 km. To these values has to be added the Giotto spacecraft position uncertainty                                            ) Tj
T* (of 80-100 km to give the overall targeting uncertainty. The resulting uncertainty of                                              ) Tj
T* (200-300 km is still relatively large compared with the intended flyby distance of 500                                             ) Tj
